# Copy this to `hooks.cfg` next to hookdll.dll on the XP box to override
# which hooks are enabled / exit-hooked, without a rebuild. Not required --
# if hooks.cfg is missing, the built-in defaults apply (every confirmed
# address enabled with entry+exit, EXCEPT:
#   - the two `approximate: true` addresses -- fugc_mode_dispatch,
#     tlb_f135_poly_remap -- disabled until you've verified them live;
#   - tlb_polypixel -- SEE THE CORRECTION BELOW. This list used to say it is
#     "disabled by default"; IT IS NOT, and never has been. Its flags are
#     0,1,0,0,0 (HEAD: 0,0,0,0,0) -- hotPathDisabled is not set on it, unlike
#     sba_vm_interp which really does carry it. The intent was clearly that it
#     should be off, and the reasoning below still holds, but the flag was
#     never added, so the built-in default ENABLES it. It is a per-PIXEL hot
#     path, so if you want it off you must say so explicitly in hooks.cfg;
#     both shipped capture configs (hooks.cfg.framing, hooks.cfg.reference)
#     do. Found 2026-08-21 by check_hooks_cfg.py, which resolves the real
#     defaults out of the table rather than trusting this comment.
#   - five `notCallReachable` addresses -- sba_set_shifts_12,
#     icc_effect_op_ctor, tla_baddscene, tla_colneg_planar_scan,
#     tla_colneg_mmx_kernel -- disabled because a 2026-08-15 r2 audit found
#     these are NOT independently call-reachable function entries (internal
#     branch targets inside a different, larger function, or -- for
#     tla_baddscene -- suspected but not fully confirmed either way).
#     UNLIKE the other two categories, there is nothing to verify live that
#     would make it safe to turn these back on: enabling one of these VAs
#     via hooks.cfg re-introduces the exact stack-corruption mechanism that
#     (most likely) caused this harness's real "stops mid-loop under load,
#     no shutdown message" failures on 2026-08-14 -- do not re-enable any
#     of these five without first re-deriving a genuinely different,
#     call-reachable address for whatever they were meant to observe.
# See ../README.md for the full hook table and per-address citations.
#
# Syntax:
#   # comment
#   EXIT=on|off              global default for entry+exit vs entry-only
#   <hook_id>=on|off          enable/disable one hook entirely
#   <hook_id>.exit=on|off     entry+exit vs entry-only for one hook
#
# Example: the two approximate-address hooks are OFF by default -- if
# you've confirmed fugc_mode_dispatch's real entry address from a live
# disassembly on the XP box and want to try it:
#
# fugc_mode_dispatch=on

# tlb_polypixel is OFF by default (unlike everything else that isn't
# `approximate: true`) -- NOT because its address is in doubt (docs/74
# SS32.2 disassembled it directly: a completely ordinary, safe-to-hook
# function), but because it's a confirmed real per-pixel hot path AND the
# live-capture question it was originally hooked to answer (a naming
# ambiguity with tlb_f135_poly_remap) has since been fully resolved by
# that same static disassembly -- see hookcore_real_table.c's citation for
# the full reasoning. If you have a NEW reason to trace it live, turn on
# just entry markers first, not full entry+exit:
#
# tlb_polypixel=on
# tlb_polypixel.exit=off
#
# v46 UPDATE to the paragraph above: tlb_polypixel is ON by default again
# (it was re-enabled 2026-08-16 for the poly_input_r dump) and as of v46 it
# is exit-hooked too, because the reference scan measures it at only 77
# calls for a whole roll -- the "per-pixel hot path" reading was about the
# loop INSIDE the call, not the call rate. The entry+exit pair of its
# 0x84000 buffer is stage 2's input and output, in place.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# v46 additions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# sba_vm_interp is now OFF by default (hotPathDisabled). It fires 185,329
# times in one reference scan -- more than every other hook combined -- and
# the question it was hooked for is answered (docs/74 SS88 ported the
# interpreter). Its dump rows are capped at 4 each in v46, so re-enabling it
# is survivable now, but it will still add ~90 MB of bare enter lines:
#
# sba_vm_interp=on
#
# ---- TLB.dll framing hooks -------------------------------------------
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-21 (docs/74 SS189.4). An earlier version of this block told
# you NOT to enable these, on two stated premises. BOTH WERE FALSE:
#
#   "TLB.dll is not present on the build machine" -- it is, at
#   /tmp/pakon_re/TLB.dll, md5 193d9b2ce0a4b77ae9b78262bd06c0fc. The search
#   behind that claim was scoped to the repo, and mdfind does not index /tmp.
#
#   "the warning-bit sites imply a range containing the framing entry, so
#   0x100072c0 may be interior" -- reading the DLL dissolves it. The driver
#   fcn.10006e70 is 0x10006e70-0x100072b5 and the entry fcn.100072c0 is
#   0x100072c0-0x100079b1: ADJACENT, 11 bytes of padding, NOT nested. Three
#   of the four `or` sites are inside the driver; the fourth (0x10007d35) is
#   in fcn.100079c0, the outer caller.
#
# All three are now re-derived (r2 af/afi/axt against that md5), confirmed as
# real function entries with prologues that take a 5-byte patch without
# splitting an instruction and no jump targets in the patched bytes, and are
# approximate=0 -- i.e. tlb_framing_entry and tlb_framing_driver are ON by
# default. The cascade below them is bit-exact at 1,429 checks (SS194).
#
# tlb_framing_line_reduce remains OFF by default, on LOG VOLUME alone --
# hotPathDisabled, not doubt. It is per-LINE: its dump row is capped at 6, but
# every call still costs one enter line and the threshold search re-runs the
# extractor, so budget tens of MB. Turn it on with:
#
# tlb_framing_line_reduce=on
#
# THAT ROW IS THE POINT OF A FRAMING CAPTURE. It dumps the vendor's own 8-bit
# per-line RGB summary at this+0x6c -- the one input the ported cascade cannot
# be fed from anything currently in the tree, and therefore the only thing
# still standing between FRAMING_PORTED and True (SS194.5).
#
# Still worth running framing as its own scan rather than alongside the colour
# reference trace -- not for safety now, but for volume: the reference trace is
# ~175 MB before line_reduce adds to it.
